      "english": "In Proposition II.13, the comparative predicate ἔλαττόν ἐστι carries two acute accents because the enclitic copula ἐστι forces an accent back onto the final syllable of ἔλαττον. When we compare this to Book I Definition 12, where it defines an acute angle as ἐλάσσων ὀρθῆς, we see the spelling uses a double sigma rather than the double tau found in Book II. That variation between double sigma and double tau is a well-attested dialectal feature across the Greek mathematical corpus.",
